# Agent Guidelines

## Problem-Solving Approach
- **Plan before acting** - Understand the problem fully before proposing solutions
- **Root cause analysis** - Identify why issues occur, not just symptoms
- **Familiarise with the codebase** - Study existing patterns, styles, and conventions before making changes
- **Keep PRs small and focused** - When working on a branch, keep the changes focused on a single issue or feature.

## Code Style
- **Remove all comments** - The default is to remove all comments, all the time; a well-written file has no comments. Load the `writing-comments` skill when you add or update a comment. First make the code say it through a name, a value, or a structure. Keep a comment only in the rare case the skill allows, and only to explain why the code exists in that form, never what it does.
- **Follow existing conventions** - Match the style already present in the codebase
- **Before writing a new file**, read 2-3 existing files in the same package. Match their comment style, naming conventions, section structure, and level of documentation exactly. Do not write "generic good code" — write code that looks like it belongs in this specific codebase.

## Writing And Documentation
- **Use ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (STE)** - Load the `simplified-technical-english` skill before writing or editing project text, including code comments, README files, documents, guides, help text, templates, and project instructions.
- **Keep comments narrow** - See the `writing-comments` skill for the full rule, including the Go rules on package comments and name narration.
- **Keep project documents task-focused** - Use short, direct sentences, consistent terminology, and explicit steps. Prefer the repo's existing commands and terms over new wording.
- **One sentence per line in Markdown** - When producing or editing Markdown, start each sentence on a new line. Do not wrap a sentence across lines, and do not put two sentences on one line. This keeps diffs to the sentence that changed, so a review comment anchors to one idea. Applies to prose and list items. A table row stays on one line, because a cell cannot hold a line break, and code blocks keep their own formatting.
- **Use the glossary** - When the repo has a `GLOSSARY.md` at its root, it defines the domain terms and verbs. Use each term with the spelling and meaning shown there. Add new domain terms to it in the same change.

## Development Process
Work from the requirements to the code, in this order.
Do not start a step until I agree the step before it.
The system tests are the guide: they show that each user flow does what the requirements promise.

1. **Requirements** - Write the high-level requirements and the user flows that use them.
   Give each requirement and each flow an ID, so that a test case can name it.
2. **Functional specification** - For each requirement, describe the behaviour: the inputs, the outputs, the errors, and the edge cases.
3. **Architecture decisions** - Record each decision and its reason in an ADR before you write the code that depends on it.
4. **Test case design** - Design the functional and system test cases from the requirements and the user flows.
   Link each case to the requirements and flows that it verifies.
   Write the system tests before the implementation where you can.
5. **Implementation** - Write the code that makes the system tests pass.
   Add unit tests to check the rules inside the code.
   A unit test supports a system test, and does not replace it.

- **Keep the trace current** - When a change adds or changes a promise, change its requirement and its test case in the same change.
- **Scale the process to the change** - For a small fix, update only the steps that the fix changes.
- **Flag test changes** - If existing tests need modification, highlight this explicitly
- **Clean Architecture projects** - Use dependency injection and mocks for the unit tests

## Languages & Tools (GitHub / Personal Projects)
- **Languages**: Go, Bash, PowerShell, JavaScript
- **Build tool**: Make - drive all commands/tasks through Makefile targets
- **Version control**: Git and GitHub CLI (prefer `gh api graphql` for CI/PR information)

## Workflow

- **Use Make** (in GitHub repos) - check the `Makefile` for commands before defaulting to other methods.
- **Always run linters and tests** before considering work complete
- **Never commit on my behalf** - I review and commit incrementally myself
- **Incremental progress** - Complete one aspect fully before moving to the next

## VS Code Remote Navigation

- **Use `code --goto` when opening files for me** - Prefer `code -r --goto /abs/path/file:line` over plain file links for reliable cursor jumps in remote VS Code sessions
- **Navigate one file at a time on request** - When walking through an issue, open one target, wait for confirmation, then move to the next
- **Prefer `path:line` references in chat** - Provide jump targets as `/abs/path/file:line` so I can paste directly into quick-open or terminal
- **Create files before opening with `--goto`** - If a file will be newly created, write it to disk first and only then run `code -r --goto /abs/path/file:line` to avoid stale/empty unsaved tabs
- **Recover from stale editors explicitly** - If an opened tab looks empty or out of sync, close that tab and reopen from disk with `code -r --goto /abs/path/file:line`; if needed use "Compare Active File with Saved"

## GitHub And CI Skills

- **Use `using-github-cli` for GitHub tasks** - For PR checks, reviews, comments, GraphQL queries, and host-aware `gh` workflows
- **Keep AGENTS as routing policy, keep command details in skills** - Avoid duplicating long command examples here; update the skill docs instead

